WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.639
Modern scientific inquiry relies on a
precise structural foundation, research

2
00:00:05.639 --> 00:00:06.639
ethics.

3
00:00:06.699 --> 00:00:10.260
This is the load-bearing architecture of
the entire scientific method.

4
00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.439
This framework, developed by the BRIGRET
project,

5
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:15.000
maps out those boundaries.

6
00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.620
Co-funded by the European Union, this
initiative is designed

7
00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:23.780
to bridge critical gaps in current ethics training
and education across the scientific community.

8
00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:28.159
This diagram outlines the framework's
target audience, dividing the community

9
00:00:28.159 --> 00:00:30.180
into two distinct, parallel tracks.

10
00:00:30.399 --> 00:00:33.959
The top branch focuses on Research Ethics
Committee members, or

11
00:00:33.959 --> 00:00:34.919
RECs.

12
00:00:34.959 --> 00:00:38.279
It provides foundational project review
training for new members, while

13
00:00:38.279 --> 00:00:41.659
equipping experienced ones with the tools
to oversee specialized, emerging

14
00:00:41.659 --> 00:00:43.259
fields like artificial intelligence.

15
00:00:43.319 --> 00:00:45.819
The lower branch follows the researchers
themselves.

16
00:00:45.819 --> 00:00:50.599
It traces a progression from early-career
PhDs learning to identify ethical concerns,

17
00:00:51.139 --> 00:00:55.139
up to senior scientists managing complex
data and interdisciplinary challenges.

18
00:00:55.639 --> 00:00:59.500
Despite these different paths, both tracks
converge on a shared

19
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:03.500
set of core objectives, primarily the need
to balance scientific

20
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.180
freedom against external societal
interests.

